{
  "an7.54:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 7.54 ",
  "an7.54:0.2": "6. The Undeclared Points ",
  "an7.54:0.3": "The Undeclared Points ",
  "an7.54:1.1": "Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: ",
  "an7.54:1.2": "“Sir, what is the cause, what is the reason why a learned noble disciple has no doubts regarding the undeclared points?” ",
  "an7.54:2.1": "“Mendicant, it’s due to the cessation of views that a learned noble disciple has no doubts regarding the undeclared points. ",
  "an7.54:2.2": "‘A realized one still exists after death’: this is a misconception. ",
  "an7.54:2.3": "‘A realized one no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. ",
  "an7.54:2.4": "‘A realized one both still exists and no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. ",
  "an7.54:2.5": "‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death’: this is a misconception. ",
  "an7.54:2.6": "An unlearned ordinary person doesn’t understand views, their origin, their cessation, or the practice that leads to their cessation. ",
  "an7.54:2.7": "And so their views grow. They’re not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re not freed from suffering, I say. ",
  "an7.54:3.1": "A learned noble disciple does understand views, their origin, their cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation. ",
  "an7.54:3.2": "And so their views cease. They’re freed from rebirth, old 